# Searching

#### Imagine opening a school website and typing **“calendar”** into the search bar. Instantly, a short list of pages appears even though the site contains hundreds of documents.

<br>

<img src="https://images.code.org/09f3b6ac8f017d7cae84610b797af6ad-image-1767801900953.png" alt="example of a search bar with results" width="300px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 40px 10px 20px;">

<img src="https://images.code.org/59c9b8a356ff4e56ec8c802ba5718b37-image-1684269154206.png" alt="A clipboard with the words TO DO on it. This icon is used to signal an action item for participants." width="30px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0px 10px 10px 0px;">

#### **Pause & Reflect**

- #### How does the website decide which pages match your search?
- #### What happens if a page *almost* matches your word, but not exactly?
- #### Who might be helped, or left out, by how search results are ordered?

<br>

> #### You may have thought about things like: keywords matter, spelling matters, some results appear first, and some content may never show up at all.

#### 👉 The results were created using a **searching algorithm**.
---

## What is a searching algorithm?

#### A **searching algorithm** is a rule a program uses to look through data to find items that match a specific value or keyword.

#### In JavaScript, searching is often used to scan arrays or strings to find matching data — like locating a student name, a message, or a product based on what a user types.

---

## What is a query?

#### When you use a search feature, you are asking the computer a question — even if it doesn’t feel like one.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">🔍 <strong>Query</strong> is the question or request you send to a computer to get specific information back from a larger set of data.</p>
</div>

#### A query might be:
- #### A word you type into a search bar  
- #### A phrase like a student name or title  
- #### A value the program is trying to find inside a list  

#### The searching algorithm uses the query to decide **what counts as a match** and what does not.

<br>



---

## Next up

#### Let’s take a look at how searching works in JavaScript and what “matching” really means behind the scenes.


